
Title: "Mathlingua by Example"
Id: "ad838b0d-69fe-454b-b230-63e2e94ab18c"


Text: "The Describes: construct is used to specify an abstract concept, called a
       type in other languages. To start, we specify that a set is an type.
       We'll expand on it as we continue.

       Here is the minimal content needed to define an abstract concept in
       Mathlingua.

       ```mlg
       [\set]
       Describes: X
       Documented:
       . called: \"set\"
       Id: \"a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1\"
       ```

       The `\set` specifies the name used to identify this type. The
       `Describes:` construct is used to define abstract concepts, i.e. types.

       The `Id:` is used to specify a unique ID that identifies this item. Note
       that you do not need to specify this ID manually. If you omit the `Id:`
       section entirely and run `mlg check`, an `Id:` section with a new ID will
       be automatically generated for you."
Id: "c580c071-1b61-4413-960d-83493009a585"


SectionTitle: "The Documented Section"
Id: "e15e88c6-675c-4b51-83ce-d53c9469c56e"


Text: "The `Documented:` section is used to document information about the
       concept being defined. Either a `called:` or a `written:` item needs to
       be specified.

       The `called:` section's text is interpreted as non-math mode LaTeX and
       allows you to specify what the construct is called when spoken aloud.
       More than one item can be specified if there is more than one way to
       describe the item.

       The `written:` section's text is interpreted as math mode LaTeX and
       allows you to specify what the construct looks like when written on
       paper.

       If only one of `written:` or `called:` is provided, the other item is
       automatically generated as the same value. So in the example above
       `called:` specifies that one says \"set\" when specifying the construct
       and because `written:` is omitted, one would also write \"set\".

       To make this more clear, consider the following example:

       ```mlg-fragment
       [\function:on{A}:to{B}]
       Describes: f(x__) ::= y_
       ...
       Documented:
       . called: \"function on $A?$ to $B?$\"
       . written: \"f? \: : \: A? \rightarrow B?\"
       ...
       ```

       We'll gloss over some details here, but at a high level, a type name can
       have inputs, here `A` and `B`, and a `?` is used to refer to them in the
       `Documented:` section.

       Here the `called:` and `written:` sections communicate that if one had `g
       is \function:on{X}:to{Y}`, then to describe that out loud one would say
       \"g is a function on $X$ to $Y$\". To express the same thing written
       down, one would write $g \: : \: X \rightarrow Y$. Further uses of
       `Documented:` will be covered in more detail later."
Id: "b85750a7-1af6-4208-bbf5-e77b939b9f7a"


SectionTitle: "The Requires Section"
Id: "3e4c3873-58cf-483a-a116-eac0d7579da0"


Text: "Now we've started to describe a set, but we need to communicate that a
       set has *set membership* capability. We do that with the `Requires:`
       section.

       ```mlg
       [\set]
       Describes: X
       Requires:
       . capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> \\abstract
       Documented:
       . called: \"set\"
       Id: \"a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1\"
       ```

       The `Requires:` is where you communicate that a concept has additional
       capabilities that introduce new symbols or are operations (like `*` for
       example).

       In Mathlingua one writes `X is \set` to introduce the new symbol `X` and
       specify it is of type is a `\set`. The form `<name> \"op\" <symbol>` is
       called a specification and can be thought of as a function that reduces
       down to an `<name> is <type>` form.

       For example, if `X` is a set of `\real` numbers than `\"in\"` could be
       defined such that `x \"in\" X` reduces to `x is \real`.

       Here we say that a set has the concept of `\"in\"` which represents set
       membership and the definition of the membership is on the right hand side
       of `:->`. The built-in `\\abstract` represents that the specification is
       abstract an sub-types of `\set` need to fill in the details.

       Now we can state the definition of a set:"
Id: "528fa26e-21c1-4d06-b8ae-5fcf7be3fa41"


[\set]
Describes: X
satisfies:
. \axiom.of.empty.set
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Documented:
. called: "set"
Id: "a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1"


SectionTitle: "Defining Concrete Instances"
Id: "b6865f01-8e1a-4cbc-aa9f-468e4d61f2e5"


Text: "Now we have the start of an (incomplete) definition of a set which is an
       abstract concept. By that I mean, a \"set\" is a name describing a whole
       collection of concrete objects that are each \"sets\". `Describes:` is
       the mechanism to describe such a type.

       A concrete object, on the other hand, is a specific instance of an type
       and a `Defines:` is used to define such concrete instances.

       For example, we can define the specific set, the empty set.

       ```mlg
       [\empty.set]
       Defines: X is \set
       expresses:
       . not:
         . exists: Y is \set
           suchThat: Y \"in\" X
       Documented:
       . called: \"empty set\"
       . written: \"\emptyset\"
       Id: \"96908ddf-5fc5-455d-9cfd-eaf0fd6ada38\"
       ```

       Here `\empty.set` is the identifier for an empty set, and the `Defines: X
       is \set` section specifies that `X` is the interal symbol that represents
       the empty set and that an empty set is a set.

       Next the `expresses:` section defines the characteristics of the empty
       set, i.e. it is a set that contains no other sets, and again we document
       how the empty set is spoken and written."
Id: "5cf63c26-9162-448d-ad46-a09810ea5a14"


[\empty.set]
Defines: X is \set
expresses:
. not:
  . exists: Y is \set
    suchThat: Y "in" X
Documented:
. called: "empty set"
. written: "\emptyset"
Id: "96908ddf-5fc5-455d-9cfd-eaf0fd6ada38"


SectionTitle: "Stating Axioms"
Id: "e526662a-8348-4df8-b106-f91cd75bf05f"


Text: "Now that we have described the abstract concept of a set, and have
       defined a concrete set, we can express one of the axioms of
       Zermelo-Frankel set theory that enables that set to exist.

       ```mlg
       [\axiom.of.empty.set]
       Axiom:
       then:
       . exists: E is \set
         suchThat: E = \empty.set
       Id: \"f2180719-568d-4084-982b-a71c5f195bf6\"
       ```

       With that, we can update our definition of a `\set` to indicate that a
       set must satisfy the axiom of empty sets.

       ```mlg
       [\set]
       Describes: X
       satisfies:
       . \axiom.of.empty.set
       Requires:
       . capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> \\abstract
       Documented:
       . called: \"set\"
       Id: \"a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1\"
       ```

       Now this definition of a set is incomplete because there are many more
       axioms in Zermelo-Frankel set theory, but this document isn't meant to be
       exhaustive but instead give an introduction to describing concepts in
       Mathlingua. As such, we won't add the other axioms to focus on more
       aspects of Mathlingua."
Id: "e798d1a3-1029-44f3-8b92-d794cbb6596c"


[\axiom.of.empty.set]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: E is \set
  suchThat: E = \empty.set
Id: "f2180719-568d-4084-982b-a71c5f195bf6"


SectionTitle: "Infix Describes"
Id: "ea698ea7-0ce5-4037-bfbb-4c692840211b"


Text: "In mathematics, sometimes what looks like an infix operator actually
       introduces a new symbol. For example, if $B$ is a set and one says let
       $A \subset B$, what they mean is $A$ is a set and is specifically a set
       whose elements all are elements of $B$.

       Infix Describes allow one to communicate this in Mathlingua:

       ```mlg
       [A \:subset:/ B]
       Describes: A
       when: B is \set
       extends: A is \set
       satisfies:
       . forAll: X \"in\" A
         then: X \"in\"? B
       Requires:
       . capability: x_ \"in\" A :-> x_ \"in\" B
       Documented:
       . called: \"$A?$ is a subset of $B?$\"
       . written: \"A? \subset B?\"
       Id: \"2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e\"
       ```

       A infix describes is of the form `<symbol> \:...:/ <expression>` and the
       left hand side of the `\:...:/` must match exactly the symbol introduced
       in `Describes: ...`.

       Here the `when:` section specifies that the argument `B` must satisfy `B
       is \set`. Further, the `extends: A is \set` specifies that by saying `A
       \:subset:/ B` one is saying that `A is \set` with additional properties.

       Those additional properties are communicated in the `satisfies:` section.
       Specifically that $A \subset B$ means every element of $A$ is an element
       of $B$."
Id: "c5553e55-f2d7-47eb-801c-30f599bfd0f1"


SectionTitle: "The Role of Requires in the Checker"
Id: "2d84b63a-b5f7-4513-9b96-4a48c49465c5"


Text: "That constraint is also defined in a `Requires:` section because that
       allows the Mathlingua type checker to understand how a statement like `x
       \"in\" X` should be treated if `X \subset Y`.

       For example, with this, if one writes `X \:subset:/ \reals` and `\reals`
       is defined such that `x \"in\" \reals` means `x is \real`, then if one
       writes `x \"in\" X` the checker would know that means `x \"in\" \reals`
       which means `x is \real`. This is needed because the details in the
       satisfies section is opaque to the checker."
Id: "2d77425a-cc1e-443b-87d2-295002e8741f"


SectionTitle: "Specifications and Statements"
Id: "72f99da4-088d-472d-82a1-10907be96d7e"


Text: "In the `satisfies:` section, you'll notice two ways \"in\" is used.
       First, it is used as `forAll: X \"in\" A` and later as `X \"in\"? B`
       (note the `?`).

       The first `X \"in\" A` is a *specification* and introduces a symbol `X`
       that is an element of `A`. Every specification automatically as an
       associated statement form specified by using the `?` suffix. In this case
       the statement `X \"in\"? B` represents the question of whether or not `X`
       is an element of `B`.

       Similar to `\"op\"` specifications, infix describes also have an
       associated statement form automatically available for them. For example,
       for the `A \:subset:/ B` infix describes we defined that introduces, `A`
       as a set that is a subset of `B`, one can write `A \:subset?:/ C` which
       represents the statement that asks if `A` is a subset of `C`.

       Thus specifications introduce symbols, and statements communicate
       questions that may be true or false about symbols that already exist."
Id: "caec325a-d0c5-49d4-b1fd-63699ff1247a"


[A \:subset:/ B]
Describes: A
when: B is \set
extends: A is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: X "in" A
  then: X "in"? B
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" A :-> x_ "in" B
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is a subset of $B?$"
. written: "A? \subset B?"
Id: "2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e"


SectionTitle: "The States Construct"
Id: "25a263ac-98ce-4a39-81b6-268cac8a4f9a"


Text: "Above we discussed how a specification has an associated statement form,
       but you can also create statements explicitly using the `States:`
       construct.

       For example, the following communciates the statement that $x$ is not an
       element of a set $X$.

       ```mlg
       [x \.not.in./ X]
       States:
       when: x, X is \set
       that:
       . not: x \"in\"? X
       Documented:
       . written: \"x? \notin X?\"
       Id: \"756e0f48-1c0e-4e05-b97a-9c4fcd577603\"
       ```

       The `<arg> \. ... ./ <arg>` syntax is used to specify a statement and
       here we specify what the statement means in the `that:` section.

       Again note that statements do *not* introduce new symbols but instead
       make statements about existing symbols.

       Thus one **cannot** write:
       ```mlg-fragment
       exists: x \.not.in./ X
       suchThat: ...
       ```

       but one could write:
       ```mlg-fragment
       exists: x is \set
       suchThat: x \.not.in./ X
       ```
       That is, `x` needs to first be introduced and then a statement about it
       can be expressed.

       With the `States:` construct we can write more compact forms of
       statements. For example, consider the following:

       ```mlg
       [P \.iff./ Q]
       States:
       when: P, Q is \\statement
       that:
       . have: P
         iff: Q
       Documented:
       . written: \"P? \iff Q?\"
       Id: \"e0ad7367-d31b-433a-8a9c-c631d59a2632\"
       ```

       Here we made a shorthand for expressing a statement `P` is true if and
       only if a statement `Q` is true. The builtin `\\statement` is the type of
       statements.

       With the above, one could write `(x \"in\"? X) \.iff./ (x \"in\"? Y)` for
       example.

       Note that the signature of a `States:` doesn't have to be of the form
       `<arg> \. ... ./ <arg>`. For example consider the following,

       ```mlg
       [\if{P}:then{Q}]
       States:
       when: P, Q is \\statement
       that:
       . if: P
         then: Q
       Documented:
       . written: \"P? \Rightarrow Q?\"
       Id: \"e1d413e6-767b-4b1f-a77c-64a169fb70d3\"
       ```
       which represents if `P` implies `Q`.

       All of the above statements are included below:"
Id: "716d3f0d-e78c-432d-8670-e1df4a71f0ee"


[x \.not.in./ X]
States:
when: x, X is \set
that:
. not: x "in"? X
Documented:
. written: "x? \notin X?"
Id: "756e0f48-1c0e-4e05-b97a-9c4fcd577603"


[P \.iff./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. have: P
  iff: Q
Documented:
. written: "P? \iff Q?"
Id: "e0ad7367-d31b-433a-8a9c-c631d59a2632"


[\if{P}:then{Q}]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. if: P
  then: Q
Documented:
. written: "P? \Rightarrow Q?"
Id: "e1d413e6-767b-4b1f-a77c-64a169fb70d3"


SectionTitle: "Theorems"
Id: "39fb9b40-69ca-4661-adc5-96c42433d5e4"


Text: "Now we have the machinery in place to describe our first theorem, which
       is a statement that can be proven.

       ```mlg
       Theorem:
       given:
       . C is \set
       . B \:subset:/ C
       . A \:subset:/ B
       then:
       . A \:subset?:/ C
       Id: \"6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720\"
       ```

       The `Theorem:` construct is used to communicate theorems, where the
       `given:` section represents the *inputs* to the theorem and the `then:`
       section describes the results of the theorem."
Id: "2fe63f47-40c1-4850-ad2f-d711ac2741e2"


Theorem:
given:
. C is \set
. B \:subset:/ C
. A \:subset:/ B
then:
. A \:subset?:/ C
Id: "6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720"


Text: "Sometimes theorems have results that are true if and only if certain
       assumptions are true. Mathlingua supports this with the optional `iff:`
       section as follows:

       ```mlg
       Theorem:
       given: A, B is \set
       then: A = B
       iff:
       . A \:subset?:/ B
       . B \:subset?:/ A
       Id: \"d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100\"
       ```

       Another aspect to notice here is that we can write `A, B is \set` instead
       of
       ```mlg-fragment
       given:
       . A is \set
       . B is \set
       ```
       although specifying `A` and `B` is valid syntax too."
Id: "35531180-accc-4a7e-a2c4-71fea1fbd3f1"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: A = B
iff:
. A \:subset?:/ B
. B \:subset?:/ A
Id: "d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100"


SectionTitle: "Entity Structures"
Id: "865e62bb-4c3d-4fc7-8a72-6d503211c67b"


Text: "So far, our definitions have used `Describes: X`. That is, our
       definitions do not specify a shape the entity being defined could have.

       Mathlingua supports the following structures:

       * `f(x_)` for mappings, i.e. entities that map and input to an output
       * `(x, f(x_), (a, b)) for tuples, i.e. entities that are a fixed named
         sequence of items
       * `{x_ : ...}` for collections, i.e. entities that are a collection of
         items

       Note that `f(x_)` does not specify a *function* but instead only
       something that associates inputs to outputs. For example `f(x)` maps `x`
       to an output `f(x)` if `f` is a function, relation, etc. But also, `A(i,
       j)` could be used to represent a matrix.

       Similarly, `{x_ : ...}` doesn't necessarily mean a set but instead a
       collection. That is, it could represent a set or a class.

       That is consider the definition of a function:
       ```mlg
       [\function:on{A}:to{B}]
       Describes: f(x__) ::= y_
       when: A, B is \set
       specifies:
       . x__ \"in\" A
       . y_ \"in\" B
       satisfies:
       . forAll: x \"in\" A
         then:
         . existsUnique: y \"in\" B
           suchThat: f(x) = y
       Documented:
       . called: \"function on $A?$ to $B?$\"
       . written: \"f? \: : \: A? \rightarrow B?\"
       Id: \"1e0cb28d-bc8f-42db-bcfa-86a215e88c7a\"
       ```

       Notice that the definition describes that a `\function:on{A}:to{B}` has
       the shape of mapping an input `x__` to the output `y_`. However, that
       just describes the shape of the entity. The key characteristics of a
       function is that it maps an input to a unique output, which is described
       in the `satisfies:` section.

       If the `Describes:` is of the form, `Describes: f(x_) ::= y_` then one
       can write `f(x)`. That is, `f` must have exactly one input. If the
       `Describes:` was instead `Describes: f(x_, y_) ::= z_` then one could
       write `f(x, y)` but not `f(x)`. That is, the mapping is called with
       exactly two arguments.

       Here a symbol with a trailing `_` indicates a placeholder. This makes it
       unambiguous to specify a mapping from a mapping's value. That is, `f(x_)`
       represents a mapping `f` that takes one input, denoted `x_`. Note that
       the name of the input doesn't matter. One could also write `f(t_)`.

       On the other hand `f(x)` represents the value of `f` when applied to `x`.
       Here the name `x` does matter, because `f(x)` only makes sense if the
       symbol `x` is already defined.

       Here, double trailing `_` indicates a *magnetic* placeholder. That is,
       `f(x__)` means treat `f` as taking a single that is referenced as `x__`
       but `f` can be written with multiple inputs that are converted to a
       tuple.

       An example will make this more clear. Consider `f(x__) ::= y_`. Then if
       one writes `f(a, b)` then the call is treated like `f((a, b))`, i.e. a
       single tuple input with `x__ := (a, b)`. For `f(a, b, c)` then the call
       is treated like `f((a, b, c))`, i.e. `x__ := (a, b, c)`. Last, since a
       tuple with one value is treated in Mathlingua as the value itself, `f(a)`
       is treated like `f((a))` but `(a)` and `a` mean the same.

       As a result, using `f(x__)` in the `Describes:` for our definition of a
       function allows us to talk about a function as if it only has one input,
       and still support multiple argument functions.

       This way of describing multiple argument functions aligns with how multi
       argument functions are classically specified in mathematical literature."
Id: "c4eaa2cf-c945-47b8-a7bd-4d88b7834ba8"


[\function:on{A}:to{B}]
Describes: f(x__) ::= y_
when: A, B is \set
specifies:
. x__ "in" A
. y_ "in" B
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y "in" B
    suchThat: f(x) = y
Documented:
. called: "function on $A?$ to $B?$"
. written: "f? \: : \: A? \rightarrow B?"
Id: "1e0cb28d-bc8f-42db-bcfa-86a215e88c7a"
